issue=f'''<!-- P1 ISSUE START --><section id="systems" class="panel" aria-labelledby="systems-title"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">3P.1 · CURRENT-CONFIGURATION RECONCILIATION · 19 OCTOBER 2026</p><h1 id="systems-title">Hotel power, cabin heat and air brakes: one coordinated budget.</h1><p>Completed audit/calculation issue, not completed Phase3P or an electrical installation drawing. Exact original3F inputs, calculator and results are preserved and hashed. {len(replay)} numeric/component comparisons replay the original six thermal cases within floating-point precision.</p></div>
<article id="systems-correction" class="card build-notice"><h2>The old hydraulic-pump allowance is not a current train load</h2><p>Original3F contained two24V/20A hydraulic pumps per coach at10% duty:96W average and960W simultaneous pump running, before other devices. They are <strong>excluded from the current air-brake schedule</strong>. Older files remain historical evidence. Do not reuse their pump-start or10A-average battery results as current air-brake sizing.</p><p>The remaining6A is still only an inherited allowance, not measured or selected air-brake hardware. At the old60% usable-energy probe, one-hour capacity changes mathematically from16.67Ah (10A) to10Ah (6A). Neither is a battery selection. Emergency-light reserve remains separate. Exact current devices, charger losses/recharge, cold/aging, voltage and peaks remain open.</p><p><strong>No compressor load is silently set to zero.</strong> D1 has32coach combination chambers plus the separately matched locomotive. The saved catalog-volume interface remains33.5607L service and50.3411L spring for the coaches, but its basis is unresolved: those are not accepted free-air consumption or reservoir sizes. Compressor drive/duty/recovery and starting must enter either the traction-auxiliary or electrical budget once, not neither or both. The inherited10kW traction-auxiliary probe does not prove compressor capacity.</p></article>
<article id="systems-heat" class="card"><h2>Electrical input is not automatically cabin heat</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Inherited per-coach allowance</th><th>Input W</th><th>Current evidence status</th></tr></thead><tbody>{auxrows}</tbody></table></div><p>Total1080W per coach is an allocation, not actual installed input. The old thermal model used only200W inside-equipment heat. New probes distinguish200W predecessor,420W direct lights/fan/controls into cabin, and1080W if all allocated input ultimately becomes cabin heat. The420W case assumes charger/DC heat is elsewhere; fan work must not already be included in the selected HVAC net-capacity convention. None is accepted cabin heat.</p><p>Charger input = operating DC load + battery charging + conversion losses. Place each loss/load at its real location; energy stored in a battery is not instant cabin heat. Do not add the full charger input and downstream DC watts twice. Actual recharge demand may govern generator loading even when cabin heat is lower.</p><h3>Whole four-coach comparison at working U=1.5 and assumed cooling COP=2.5</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Inside equipment W/car</th><th>Condition</th><th>Cooling thermal kW</th><th>Coach hotel electrical kW</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows}</tbody></table></div><p>Hotel rows include4×1.080kW auxiliary allocation, not locomotive traction or the unresolved compressor.200→420W adds1.012kW total cooling demand after the inherited15% allowance, or0.405kW electrical at COP2.5. This is a corrected sensitivity, not a generator recommendation. Neither a demand coincidence rule nor a motor-start transient is resolved by these steady kW values.</p></article>
<article id="systems-basis" class="card"><h2>Retain explicit conditions; don’t promote probes into requirements</h2><p>Basis:3regular ×33 thermal occupants +1accessible ×14 =113thermal people, including one allowance/car. Regular/glass8.6297m²; accessible6.6684m².110°F outside/75°F inside cooling,30°F or20°F outside/68°F inside heating,7.5L/s/person outside air plus0.03m³/s average door leakage, and0.7/1.5/2.5W/m²K opaque-U probes are inherited assumptions, not official site design or adopted ventilation criteria. Humidity and sunlight are separate stresses; evening operation is not assumed identical to solar daytime.</p><p>The new source computes18thermal cases and33whole-consist cooling/heating rows. Winter occupied heat subtracts real-location assumed gains; before-gains heat is retained separately. Neither is complete preheat: body thermal mass, starting temperature and allowed warmup time are missing. The2×18×15in gross ducts do not establish net area, filter pressure, sound, fan power or delivered ventilation.</p><p>Current primary manual check retains the named Dometic CS variants as comparisons only:15,000Btu/h nominal; FJX3573M compressor13.6/71A rated/locked and fan2.6/6.9A; FJX5557M compressor12.8/57A and fan3.6/8.2A.115V/60Hz single phase. These do not establish watts, generator transients,110°F output, fresh air or rail suitability. Exact supplied revision/nameplate must govern. RV rooftop and rail rooftop/undercar families remain alternatives, not selected installations.</p><p><strong>Energy boundaries:</strong> the420W probe puts even the platform-light allocation inside, intentionally pending actual placement. Define whether selected HVAC input/COP and net capacity already include fans and controls; do not add the250W ventilation fan twice. Do not put compressor electrical input into cabin gains: condenser heat rejects outside. Transformer losses require their own physical location. The balanced240/480V currents in the source are arithmetic equivalents without transformer loss, not selected wiring topologies.</p></article>
<article id="systems-release" class="card"><h2>Remaining installation and acceptance work</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Interface</th><th>Accountable specialist</th><th>Evidence needed</th></tr></thead><tbody>{gaterows}</tbody></table></div><p>Farm crew can make reviewed nonenergized space mockups and record clearances. Qualified vehicle/body, HVAC, brake and electrical specialists must close supports, refrigerant handling, live distribution, fault response and safety testing. No cable, breaker, generator, battery, pressure setting or mounting bolt is selected in this audit.</p><h3>Dated primary evidence and editable records</h3><ol>{srclist}</ol><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="assets/systems-p1/calculations.json">18thermal /33consist cases and replay</a><a class="button" href="assets/systems-p1/build_issue.py">Editable calculation source</a><a class="button" href="assets/systems-p1/inputs-and-heat-placement.json">Inputs / heat-location and energy balance</a><a class="button" href="assets/systems-p1/decision-and-next.json">Correction / acceptance work / next issue</a><a class="button" href="assets/systems-p1/source-manifest.json">Exact predecessor hashes</a></div><p><strong>Next3P.2:</strong> exact HVAC installation-interface comparison: ADB/headroom, openings, service airflow, structural mounting boundaries and fresh-air path. If a dependent part is externally held, advance track/station and operations/approval coordination. All useful independent work continues; this does not complete the train, approve fabrication or qualify passenger operation.</p></article></section><!-- P1 ISSUE END -->'''
